A||||2||GWMORGAN||Bench Bleeding...||||5||
Z||000000||GWMORGAN||09-08-2001||03:45 AM||||How do I bench bleed a new clutch master cylinder?<BR>                            Thanx.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Nashco||09-08-2001||04:47 AM||||It's pretty basic.  It has a very small amount of internals to it, so it's quick.  Just fill the reservoir, hold your finger over the fluid hole gently, and press in the rod.  When you do this, fluid will seep past your finger; just hold your finger there gently so that the fluid doesn't squirt all over.  Then hold your finger tightly over the fluid hole and let the rod out.  You should only have to do this a couple times, until no air comes out of the hole anymore, just fluid.  Make sure you never get the fluid low enough so that air can enter the internals again.<P>Once you've bled the master cylinder, hold your finger over the fluid hole and be careful not to depress the rod while installing it.  Let your finger off when you're ready to install the line, and do so as quickly as possible.<P>The hard part is bleed the clutch line properly.  Archie's page has by far, the best method of doing it.  I followed it exactly, except that I pumped the slave in with my hand a few times extra, since I'd installed a new slave cylinder as well.  Here's a link:<P><A HREF="http://www.v8archie.com/arch4.htm" TARGET=_blank>Archie's Clutch Bleeding Archism</A><P>Any "standard" method of bleeding won't work, because air gets trapped in the slave cylinder.  This method saves you about 9 days worth of pedal pumping <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Good luck,<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT<P>P.S.  In the future, use the search function and look for answers to things that may be common questions.  I'm sure this question has been answered upwards of 50 times on the forum.||||reg||1
Z||000002||GWMORGAN||09-08-2001||02:35 PM||||Thanx for the info.||||reg||1
